---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout title="Data Format">
  <p>
    ArrowCrab reads a JSON file that describes a directed acyclic graph. The
    format is compact: arrays are flat-packed and strings are deduplicated
    with a shared string table.
  </p>

  <h2>Wire Format</h2>
  <pre><code>interface GraphData &#123;
  subgraphs: number[];  // flat-packed, 3 fields per record
  nodes:     number[];  // flat-packed, 3 fields per record
  edges:     number[];  // flat-packed, 4 fields per record
  strings:   string[];  // shared string table
&#125;</code></pre>

  <h3>Nodes</h3>
  <p>
    Every 3 consecutive entries in <code>nodes[]</code> form one record:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Offset</th><th>Field</th><th>Description</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr
        ><td><code>i+0</code></td><td>id</td><td>Globally unique integer ID</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td><code>i+1</code></td><td>label</td><td
          >Index into <code>strings[]</code></td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td><code>i+2</code></td><td>color</td><td
          >Index into <code>strings[]</code> (CSS color)</td
        ></tr
      >
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Edges</h3>
  <p>
    Every 4 consecutive entries in <code>edges[]</code> form one record:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Offset</th><th>Field</th><th>Description</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>i+0</code></td><td>sourceId</td><td>Source node ID</td></tr>
      <tr
        ><td><code>i+1</code></td><td>sourcePort</td><td
          >Output port index on source</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr><td><code>i+2</code></td><td>targetId</td><td>Target node ID</td></tr>
      <tr
        ><td><code>i+3</code></td><td>targetPort</td><td
          >Input port index on target</td
        ></tr
      >
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Subgraphs</h3>
  <p>
    Every 3 consecutive entries in <code>subgraphs[]</code> form one record:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Offset</th><th>Field</th><th>Description</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr
        ><td><code>i+0</code></td><td>nodeId</td><td
          >The compound node that owns this subgraph</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td><code>i+1</code></td><td>inId</td><td
          >Entry point of the subgraph</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td><code>i+2</code></td><td>outId</td><td
          >Exit point of the subgraph</td
        ></tr
      >
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Why Flat-Packed Arrays?</h3>
  <p>
    Flat arrays avoid per-record object overhead. Serializing thousands of
    <code>&#123;"id": ..., "label": ...&#125;</code> objects means repeating every key
    name. With flat arrays the parser just decodes numbers, and the deserializer
    unpacks records in a tight loop.
  </p>

  <h3>Why a String Table?</h3>
  <p>
    Labels and color strings often repeat across nodes. A shared
    <code>strings[]</code> array deduplicates them so each node just stores an
    index. This makes the payload smaller and keeps deserialization simple.
  </p>

  <h2>Invariants</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      All IDs are globally unique and topologically ordered (lower ID = earlier
      in the DAG).
    </li>
    <li>
      For every subgraph: <code>inId &lt; childId &lt; outId</code> for all children.
    </li>
    <li>
      A subgraph's <code>nodeId</code>, <code>inId</code>, and <code>outId</code>
      are all distinct. The <code>nodeId</code> must be outside the
      <code>(inId, outId)</code> range.
    </li>
    <li>
      All three subgraph IDs (<code>nodeId</code>, <code>inId</code>,
      <code>outId</code>) must appear in <code>nodes[]</code>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Topological ordering matters because the deserializer can iterate nodes in
    ID order knowing predecessors are already processed. It also lets scope
    assignment use simple range checks (<code
      >inId &lt; id &lt; outId</code
    >) instead of explicit membership sets.
  </p>

  <hr />

  <h2>In-Memory Model</h2>
  <p>
    <code>buildGraphModel</code> turns the wire format into a
    <code>GraphModel</code> with two lookup tables:
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Structure</th><th>Key</th><th>Contents</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>nodeMap</code></td>
        <td>node ID</td>
        <td>Label, color, port counts, scope assignment, child subgraph list</td
        >
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>scopeMap</code></td>
        <td>scope ID</td>
        <td
          >Node membership, edges, adjacency maps, boundary info, parent node
          reference</td
        >
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Scope Resolution</h3>
  <p>
    Each node gets assigned to its <strong>innermost</strong> enclosing subgraph
    scope using strict containment:
    <code>inId &lt; nodeId &lt; outId</code>. Entry, exit, and parent nodes
    all fall outside this range, so they belong to the enclosing scope rather
    than the subgraph they define. Entry and exit nodes are excluded from
    <code>nodeIds</code> entirely — they appear in <code>nodeMap</code> for
    port count inference but are not positioned by the layout engine.
  </p>
  <p>
    Edges follow the same rule, except one endpoint can sit on a boundary
    (which is how edges cross into or out of a subgraph).
  </p>

  <h3>Port Count Inference</h3>
  <p>
    Port counts aren't stored in the data. They're inferred from edges.
    Input port count for a node is <code>max(targetPort) + 1</code> across all
    edges targeting it; output port count is <code>max(sourcePort) + 1</code>.
    For entry and exit nodes, these port counts determine the boundary ports
    drawn on the subgraph frame.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
